what's going on everybody so in this  video i'm going to talk about the one  thing i probably get asked more than  anything else  which is what is the best college degree  or major  for a data science career now i can't  give  one single answer to this that's going  to work for absolutely everybody  but what we're going to do here is we're  going to look at a survey done by 365  data science  so they did research into 1001 data  scientists from  all around the globe over the years 2018  to 2020.  now the findings from this are really  interesting so we're going to talk about  that  then i'm going to give you some overall  principles behind  selecting a degree and just how the  knowledge from that is going to  translate into a data science job in the  first place  please note i'm not a professional  career advisor this video is for  entertainment purposes  and to hopefully give you some useful  food for thought if this is your first  time here  my name is richard subscribe to my  channel for all kinds of content just  like this if you haven't already  and hit the like button for the youtube  algorithm now if we look just at the  year 2018  notice a lot of these people are pretty  new on the job right  they have between zero and 12 months of  experience or maybe one to two years  well the most common area of study  appears to be computer science  that's pretty closely followed by stats  and math  that's followed by econ and social  sciences  and now this one actually kind of  surprised me  uh but it's pretty consistent with what  a lot of you guys are telling me  because a lot of you guys you come from  econ backgrounds and you ask does it  make more sense  to transition into another field but  appears that econ in general is one of  the most common fields for  breaking into data science then there's  a pretty noticeable drop-off  the next most common one is data science  and analysis  so that's all pretty interesting but if  we go over to the year 2019  notice there's a little bit of a drop  off for stats and math  it's fallen to the number three spot  behind econ and social sciences  and computer science is still in the  number one spot  but then things get really interesting  when we look at the year 2020  so in this year data science and  analysis  skyrockets to the number one spot as an  area of study  computer science is still number two  behind that ahead of  stats and math or econ and social  sciences  but this is really interesting and now  my first thought upon looking at this  might be well data science is such a new  field of study  it might have taken until the year 2020  just for people to get those degrees and  for that to be reflected in the  workplace  but no because even if you look at these  people with like three to five years of  experience  data science and analysis is still in  the number one spot here so  so this to me was absolutely fascinating  so that's all well and good if you're  able to get a data science degree  you're likely to develop a pretty  multi-disciplinary set of skills  that's going to pay enormous dividends  for you as you launch your career  here's the problem though so here in the  u.s  and i'm sure the situation is similar in  countries like the uk  or india for instance not all colleges  offer data science majors so i'm not  totally sure how accurate this  particular blog is  and i know it's going back almost a  couple years now  but they compiled a list of all the  major colleges they could find that  offer data science majors  and as i was scrolling through this list  there were a few  high-profile colleges that stood out to  me as  not being on this list as offering data  science majors  and some of them are pretty new like  even the school i graduated from the  university of michigan for instance  the data science program there is pretty  new going back to like  2015 or 2016. so all that to say  there's absolutely no guarantee the  school that you want to go to  or in the us whatever school happens to  be in your state  is going to offer a data science program  let's suppose  your school doesn't offer a data science  program well  what then now i actually want to start  this discussion off with an  anecdote so one of the best applied data  scientists and research scientists  i've ever known guy works at google now  his compensation is in several hundreds  of thousands of dollars a year  he actually came from a physics  background now he did get a phd  and everything but physics is not  exactly the type of degree that you  think of  the things you learn in that degree  translating directly into data science  and i think for the most part he would  probably agree with you  but here's the thing he considered that  degree to be one of the best decisions  he'd ever made  because he said the degree helped him to  problem solve  and just to critically think and solve  unstructured  problems so well and that's the thing  if you look at any of these top degrees  so  things like data science computer  science stats math  econ social sciences engineering even  in all of these degrees that's exactly  what you're doing  you're solving challenging often unclear  unstructured types of problems and  you're just building those  muscles in your brain to be able to do  that and so that's why  honestly with a lot of these degrees  you're not really going to go wrong  your path might look a little less  linear it might be a little bit  different from  others but either way if you want bad  enough to develop the skill sets for  data science and you come from  from any of these backgrounds you're  going to be able to  but then a lot of people even if they  haven't launched their careers off yet  they're familiar with the  conceptualization of data science being  at the intersection of stats  and computer science granted  communication and domain expertise work  into that too but that's neither here  nor there  so they'll ask the question which is a  better program  stats or computer science if you  absolutely have to pick one  now my answer to that one is going to be  a little bit biased because  i come from a stats background myself  but if i had to absolutely pick between  those two  i would go with the stats and the reason  for that is  i would rather be a data scientist who's  strong at stats but a little bit weaker  on computer science  than the other way around because let's  think about it  if you have that stats background you  know the fundamentals  you know models so you know different  ways to attack problems and you know how  to interpret results  sure you don't necessarily know all the  cs fundamentals  and algorithms out there but you can  pick up sql  you can pick up r or python you're going  to be pretty employable  on the other hand though if you're  strong at computer science  but you don't know statistics at all or  you're weak in that  it's easy for you to get into the  territory i like to call data  pseudoscience  you know what i mean that is you can  start misinterpreting  things not knowing the right ways to  approach problems and things can get  ugly  so again you're going to be in a good  position with either of these two but  again if you have to pick one i would go  with the stats  now obviously there are a number of  caveats behind all of this  the first one that i'm going to point  out is you should always pick a degree  in something that interests you  and something that you're going to like  so for instance with stats  if you absolutely hate stats it just  it's boring to you  that's not something that you should  probably major in and in fact  you probably want to rethink going into  data science in the first place if  that's the case for you  and then the other thing is particularly  if you're going into a data science  program  it's brand new for a lot of colleges and  universities  so try to make sure that your school is  conducting it properly  and now for this you might be able to do  this just by gleaning through all the  course requirements and descriptions  but failing that sit down with an  advisor and talk in detail about the  program  if you're going into a data science  program you do need to be learning  statistics  and i did a whole video on how much  statistics you need for data science  i'll have that linked in the description  but in short  you should be learning probability  calculations and bayes rule  the central limit theorem things like  the confidence interval and hypothesis  testing frameworks  things like how sampling structure and  bias affect  problems and then various linear  modeling approaches  you need to be getting some exposure to  sql ideally  and you should be learning one of r or  python preferably both  but if you learn only one of them that's  okay then ideally  if this program exposes you to things  like linear algebra  uh communication and human centered  design principles  as well as machine learning you're gonna  be in a good position  so at the end of the day just do your  due diligence  there are a lot of bad programs out  there  and before you go dropping tens of  thousands of dollars especially if  you're going into debt to do so  just do some research so if your school  does offer a data science program  that's probably going to be the best  single option you can pick  but there's no one path that every data  scientist  must go down and in fact i did a video  all about how  you can become a data scientist with no  college degree at all  granted a degree is going to help the  vast majority of people  and some employers do require it but it  is possible to get your foot in the door  without that college degree  so at the end of the day pick something  that you're going to enjoy  where you're learning skills that are  going to be valuable in the marketplace  whether that's in data science data  analytics or whatever else  and you're going to do just fine so  thanks for watching this video  if you enjoyed it hit the like button  please consider sharing the video  and then i'll see you all in the not so  distant future  until then richard on data

